Task 2.2 – Formulate a Greedy Hypothesis (1 Mark)
Greedy-choice rule:

Select the jobs in descending order of profit and assign each selected job to the latest available time slot on or before its deadline.

Justification:

This strategy prioritises jobs offering higher profits while placing them as late as possible within their deadlines. This preserves earlier slots for jobs with tighter deadlines, helping maximise the total profit.

Task 2.3 – Design the Algorithm (3 Marks)
Algorithm: JobSequencing

Objective: Maximise total profit by scheduling jobs within their deadlines.

Pseudocode

text
ALGORITHM JobSequencing

BEGIN

    READ N

    FOR i ← 1 TO N DO
        READ JobID[i]
        READ Deadline[i]
        READ Profit[i]
    END FOR

    MaxDeadline ← maximum value in Deadline

    FOR i ← 1 TO MaxDeadline DO
        Schedule[i] ← EMPTY
    END FOR

    TotalProfit ← 0

    SORT jobs in descending order of Profit

    FOR i ← 1 TO N DO

        FOR Slot ← MIN(Deadline[i], MaxDeadline)
                    DOWNTO 1 DO

            IF Schedule[Slot] = EMPTY THEN

                Schedule[Slot] ← JobID[i]

                TotalProfit ← TotalProfit + Profit[i]

                EXIT FOR

            END IF

        END FOR

    END FOR

    WRITE "Final Schedule"

    FOR Slot ← 1 TO MaxDeadline DO
        WRITE Slot, Schedule[Slot]
    END FOR

    WRITE "Total Profit:", TotalProfit

    RETURN Schedule, TotalProfit

END ALGORITHM
Explanation of the Algorithm
Read the job details, including job ID, deadline and profit.

Find the maximum deadline to determine the number of possible slots.

Initially mark all time slots as empty.

Sort the jobs in descending order of profit.

Select the highest-profit job first.

Check the available slots from its deadline backwards towards slot 1.

If a free slot is found, assign the job to that slot.

Add its profit to the total profit.

If no suitable slot is available, reject that job.

Display the final schedule and total profit.

